<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Brooke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1803">1803</a>–<a href="/years/1868">1868</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Rajah James Brooke</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Brooke was an English adventurer who helped the Brunei Sultanate put down a revolt near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuching">Kuching</a> and in 1841 was made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-of-sarawak">Rajah of Sarawak</a>. He founded the dynasty of the White Rajahs, which ruled Sarawak for a century. He also served as the first governor of the crown colony of Labuan from 1848.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-brooke.jpg" alt="James Brooke" width="320" height="404" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Herbert Watkins · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir-James-Brooke.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>James Brooke was born on 29 April 1803 at Bandel, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Calcutta</a>. His father, Thomas Brooke, was an English judge in the Court of Appeal at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bareilly">Bareilly</a>. He was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> at twelve for a brief schooling at Norwich School, from which he ran away, and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1819 as an ensign in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal-army">Bengal Army</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>. He fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-burmese-war">First Anglo-Burmese War</a> until he was seriously wounded in 1825. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Madras</a> in 1830 he arrived too late to rejoin his unit and resigned his commission. In 1835 he inherited 30,000 pounds, which he used to buy the schooner Royalist.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Brooke sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> in 1838 and arrived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuching">Kuching</a> in August to find an uprising against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunei">Brunei</a>. He helped the sultan's uncle Pengiran Muda Hashim crush it, and Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omar-ali-saifuddin-ii">Omar Ali Saifuddin II</a> offered him the governorship of Sarawak in 1841 in return. Brooke suppressed piracy in the region. Some Malay nobles in Brunei, unhappy with these measures, arranged the murder of Muda Hashim and his followers, and Brooke, helped by a unit of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s China Squadron, took over Brunei and restored the sultan. He was granted the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajah-of-sarawak">Rajah of Sarawak</a> on 24 September 1841, though the official declaration was made on 18 August 1842.</p><p>He reformed the administration and codified laws, and pacified the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dayak-people">Dayaks</a>, many of whom served in his forces. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> took possession of the island of Labuan on 24 December 1846, and in 1848 he became its governor and commander-in-chief. On a visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1847 he received the Freedom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">City of London</a>, became consul-general in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> and was made a Knight Commander of the Order of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a>. In 1851 he was accused of excessive force against native peoples under the guise of anti-piracy, and a commission of inquiry at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> in 1854 dismissed the charges. In 1857 he put down an uprising led by Liu Shan Bang.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Brooke died unmarried. He had acknowledged a son to his family in 1858, and in 1861 he named his nephew John Brooke Johnson Brooke as his successor. After a quarrel he deposed John and named John's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-brooke-rajah-of-sarawak">Charles Anthoni Johnson Brooke</a> instead. He died on 11 June 1868 at Burrator in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>, after three strokes in his last ten years, and was buried at St Leonard's Church at Sheepstor. The naturalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-russel-wallace">Alfred Russel Wallace</a> praised his hospitality at Sarawak. Brooke's Point on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palawan">Palawan</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> is named after him, as are several species, among them Rajah Brooke's birdwing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James Brooke” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1841 Sultan Omar Ali Saifuddin II granted the Sarawak region to the English adventurer <b>James Brooke</b>, who ruled it as rajah and steadily absorbed more Brunei rivers, ending with Limbang in 1890.</blockquote><a href="/empire/brunei-sultanate">Brunei Sultanate · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>It was made a crown colony in 1848, with <b>James Brooke</b>, the Rajah of Sarawak, as its first governor and Hugh Low as colonial secretary.</blockquote><a href="/state/crown-colony-of-labuan">Crown Colony of Labuan</a></li><li><blockquote>The English adventurer <b>James Brooke</b> helped the Brunei Sultanate put down a revolt of Malays and Bidayuh near Kuching in 1839 to 1841.</blockquote><a href="/state/raj-of-sarawak">Raj of Sarawak</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in Burrator, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=25.32,83.01&amp;year=1845" aria-label="Varanasi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6749 1232 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of James Brooke"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7199 1502h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7199 1502h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=25.32,83.01&amp;year=1845">What was here in 1845? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
